{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E3L78YAJzPWcXFxm83pqkn","type":"tool_result","content":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Provisional title:** How Accurate Is Regression-Based GATE Estimation Under Network Interference? Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Error in an Eight-Case Grid.\n\n## Central question\nIn a single randomized experiment on a network, how accurately can a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which features of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design decide when it works and when it fails?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the eight-case synthetic grid (n=3000; SBM / Barabasi-Albert / Watts-Strogatz; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), regression-GATE error is small (about 3-5% relative RMSE per case) when the fitted exposure mapping matches the mechanism, and large when it does not (saturating 0.198, count x degree 0.151, two-hop 0.452). Graph family, interference strength and cluster vs Bernoulli assignment did not degrade accuracy within the tested range. Nominal 95% intervals undercover (0.839). The frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure regression lowers mean relative RMSE to 0.126 vs 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, but leaves the two-hop case unresolved.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation: completed, outcome `qualified`, 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, 0 missing/failed episodes. No `downstream_study` block, so no downstream claims. Qualification supports reporting the measured results only, not generality or superiority.\n- Rejected: \"new estimator wins\" (only 3 scored variants, same case specs in search and final, no gate ablation, no per-case baselines); search-chronology (diary). Chosen: thesis-first characterization with the frozen estimator as the studied method. Not edit mode, so prior-paper status is \"rejected\" (nothing to preserve).\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure-regression estimator (proposed/studied).\n2. Plain linear exposure regression (baseline, 0.180).\n3. Difference-in-means (0.622) and neighbourhood Horvitz-Thompson (0.994) (baselines).\n4. Correctly specified oracle regression (supporting reference, 0.045).\n5. Search-plane estimator-class alternatives: flexible ridge ensemble (0.232), linear + residual correction (0.185), vs BIC-gated library (0.140) and linear (0.184) on that plane. Ablation-style, labelled search plane.\n6. Negative controls and replay (supporting): constant 9.0e-15, permuted outcome 0.0588, permuted graph RMSE 0.627, 0 vacuity violations, 0 replay diff.\n\n## Results focus\nAggregate method comparison; per-case profile with single-factor contrasts (explicitly case pairs, no per-case intervals); aggregate bias 0.074 vs RMSE 0.126; interval undercoverage (0.839, width 0.379); seed-level variability from the 24 per-seed scores (two outliers, seeds 9001 and 9015); search-plane estimator-class table, kept separate from final.\n\n## Figures (ICLR is single-column; text width about 5.5 in)\n- **Fig. 1 overview_schematic** (generate-concept-figure, full width, page 1-2): pipeline and eight-case factor grid, with the corner-extrapolation vs misspecification error sources.\n- **Fig. 2 main_accuracy** (matplotlib, full width, 2 panels): (a) five methods aggregate relative RMSE, log/broken axis; (b) per-case frozen-estimator error across 8 cases (budget relaxed to 8 categories, 1 series, because the grid has exactly eight cases).\n- **Fig. 3 reliability** (matplotlib, 0.85 width, 2 panels): per-seed scores with bootstrap interval; coverage vs 0.95 nominal.\n- Tables: case-factor table with final per-case error; search-plane estimator-class table.\n- PDF + 300 DPI PNG from one source script; LaTeX references the PDF; no shrinking of dense figures.\n\n## Body-page use (8-9 pages, no padding)\nFraming + Fig. 1 (~1), related work (~0.75), setup/estimator/metric (~1.5), results (~3-3.5), discussion and limitations (~1).\n\n## Supplement\nCase specifications; exact estimator and baseline definitions; per-seed table and bootstrap; negative controls; search-plane comparison; LLM Usage section (required by venue).\n\n## Must-disclose limitations\nSearch and final share case specifications and the library was designed after seeing search-plane per-case errors (no transfer-to-new-mechanism claim); one n, one p; case-pair contrasts, not factorial; no per-case baselines, bias or intervals; two-hop failure cause unknown (gate diagnostics unverified, so unknown); undercoverage cause not established; no public-graph or real-data experiments, no gate ablation (ablation_studies empty), no learned-graph methods.\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: Experiment A/B, search trajectory, iteration history, progression, campaign summary, lessons learned. Phrases: combined_score, correctness rate, eligible, rubric, program_1, \"we first tried\", state-of-the-art claims. Use \"model variant\", \"configuration\", \"seeded run\".\n\n## Writer notes\nCite only relevant bib entries (Chin, Aronow-Samii, Ugander, Karrer, Han-Ugander, Chen et al., Eichhorn et al., Forastiere et al., etc.) after checking provenance; many bib entries are irrelevant noise. Starter-list papers missing from the bib must not be cited without provenance. Anonymize everything. Full machine-readable plan in `paper_strategy.json`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"6e8897a0-6c9b-4849-a8f6-3214df1d50be","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:51:11.310Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Provisional title:** How Accurate Is Regression-Based GATE Estimation Under Network Interference? Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Error in an Eight-Case Grid.\n\n## Central question\nIn a single randomized experiment on a network, how accurately can a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which features of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design decide when it works and when it fails?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the eight-case synthetic grid (n=3000; SBM / Barabasi-Albert / Watts-Strogatz; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), regression-GATE error is small (about 3-5% relative RMSE per case) when the fitted exposure mapping matches the mechanism, and large when it does not (saturating 0.198, count x degree 0.151, two-hop 0.452). Graph family, interference strength and cluster vs Bernoulli assignment did not degrade accuracy within the tested range. Nominal 95% intervals undercover (0.839). The frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure regression lowers mean relative RMSE to 0.126 vs 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, but leaves the two-hop case unresolved.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation: completed, outcome `qualified`, 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, 0 missing/failed episodes. No `downstream_study` block, so no downstream claims. Qualification supports reporting the measured results only, not generality or superiority.\n- Rejected: \"new estimator wins\" (only 3 scored variants, same case specs in search and final, no gate ablation, no per-case baselines); search-chronology (diary). Chosen: thesis-first characterization with the frozen estimator as the studied method. Not edit mode, so prior-paper status is \"rejected\" (nothing to preserve).\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure-regression estimator (proposed/studied).\n2. Plain linear exposure regression (baseline, 0.180).\n3. Difference-in-means (0.622) and neighbourhood Horvitz-Thompson (0.994) (baselines).\n4. Correctly specified oracle regression (supporting reference, 0.045).\n5. Search-plane estimator-class alternatives: flexible ridge ensemble (0.232), linear + residual correction (0.185), vs BIC-gated library (0.140) and linear (0.184) on that plane. Ablation-style, labelled search plane.\n6. Negative controls and replay (supporting): constant 9.0e-15, permuted outcome 0.0588, permuted graph RMSE 0.627, 0 vacuity violations, 0 replay diff.\n\n## Results focus\nAggregate method comparison; per-case profile with single-factor contrasts (explicitly case pairs, no per-case intervals); aggregate bias 0.074 vs RMSE 0.126; interval undercoverage (0.839, width 0.379); seed-level variability from the 24 per-seed scores (two outliers, seeds 9001 and 9015); search-plane estimator-class table, kept separate from final.\n\n## Figures (ICLR is single-column; text width about 5.5 in)\n- **Fig. 1 overview_schematic** (generate-concept-figure, full width, page 1-2): pipeline and eight-case factor grid, with the corner-extrapolation vs misspecification error sources.\n- **Fig. 2 main_accuracy** (matplotlib, full width, 2 panels): (a) five methods aggregate relative RMSE, log/broken axis; (b) per-case frozen-estimator error across 8 cases (budget relaxed to 8 categories, 1 series, because the grid has exactly eight cases).\n- **Fig. 3 reliability** (matplotlib, 0.85 width, 2 panels): per-seed scores with bootstrap interval; coverage vs 0.95 nominal.\n- Tables: case-factor table with final per-case error; search-plane estimator-class table.\n- PDF + 300 DPI PNG from one source script; LaTeX references the PDF; no shrinking of dense figures.\n\n## Body-page use (8-9 pages, no padding)\nFraming + Fig. 1 (~1), related work (~0.75), setup/estimator/metric (~1.5), results (~3-3.5), discussion and limitations (~1).\n\n## Supplement\nCase specifications; exact estimator and baseline definitions; per-seed table and bootstrap; negative controls; search-plane comparison; LLM Usage section (required by venue).\n\n## Must-disclose limitations\nSearch and final share case specifications and the library was designed after seeing search-plane per-case errors (no transfer-to-new-mechanism claim); one n, one p; case-pair contrasts, not factorial; no per-case baselines, bias or intervals; two-hop failure cause unknown (gate diagnostics unverified, so unknown); undercoverage cause not established; no public-graph or real-data experiments, no gate ablation (ablation_studies empty), no learned-graph methods.\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: Experiment A/B, search trajectory, iteration history, progression, campaign summary, lessons learned. Phrases: combined_score, correctness rate, eligible, rubric, program_1, \"we first tried\", state-of-the-art claims. Use \"model variant\", \"configuration\", \"seeded run\".\n\n## Writer notes\nCite only relevant bib entries (Chin, Aronow-Samii, Ugander, Karrer, Han-Ugander, Chen et al., Eichhorn et al., Forastiere et al., etc.) after checking provenance; many bib entries are irrelevant noise. Starter-list papers missing from the bib must not be cited without provenance. Anonymize everything. Full machine-readable plan in `paper_strategy.json`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrZ4j8EJLdE2yGbSKQL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BzamSL6gsZu7RxQYRXgDZn","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.md | head -5; cat figure_manifest.json; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor f in s.get('figure_specs',[]): print({k:f[k] for k in f if k in('figure_id','figure_type','preferred_generator','preferred_span')})\nE\ngrep -n \"ht-variance\\|variance of the former\\|HT reference\\|analyzed it under\\|block bootstrap\\|block-bootstrap\\|Cluster-robust\\|rules out\\|returns the estimate\\|case 8\\|per-seed mean\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11179,"cache_read_input_tokens":40767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11179},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"92ccf912-d0f7-43f0-926c-24741ba9fe17","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:51:14.317Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrZ4B9Vvy7Mp8Ce4hPF"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BzamSL6gsZu7RxQYRXgDZn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T08:51:03.772091+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8,\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/overview_schematic.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/overview_schematic.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        5,\n        8\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"main_accuracy\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/main_accuracy.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/main_accuracy.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_linear_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_dim_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_ht_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_1\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_2\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_3\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_4\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_5\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_6\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_7\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_8\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        24,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"reliability\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 0.9,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/reliability.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/reliability.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.ci_coverage\",\n        \"final.metric.ci_width_rel\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n{'figure_id': 'overview_schematic', 'figure_type': 'diagram', 'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'main_accuracy', 'figure_type': 'bar_two_panel', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'reliability', 'figure_type': 'strip_plus_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n59:\\paragraph{Design-based estimation under interference.} Randomization-based frameworks define exposure conditions over a network and estimate their effects with inverse-probability weighting \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}; graph cluster randomization raises the probability that a unit's whole neighbourhood is treated together \\citep{Ugander2013GraphCluster,Ugander2020RandomizedGC}, and it has been deployed at scale \\citep{Karrer2020NetworkEA}. Subsequent work studies estimation theory under network interference \\citep{Sussman2017Elements}, the admissibility of HT estimation \\citep{Karwa2023Admissibility}, data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT \\citep{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}, interference with noisy or $k$-nearest-neighbour structure \\citep{Li2021Noise,Alzubaidi2023KNN}, and asymptotics for effect estimation under random-graph models of the network \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph}. Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\n65:\\paragraph{Regression adjustment and model-based estimation.} \\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals. Generalized propensity-score approaches model exposure probabilities \\citep{Forastiere2022EstimatingCE}, and \\citet{Shankar2024ABTU} and \\citet{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty} address estimation when the network is only partly known. Outside networks, regression adjustment is a variance-reduction tool in randomized experiments \\citep{Wager2016HighdimensionalRA,Ting2023Limits}. Our contribution relative to this literature is empirical: a controlled grid that separates cases where the fitted mapping matches the mechanism from cases where it does not, with explicit reference estimators, negative controls and an interval-calibration check. We do not derive new theory.\n83:\\item \\emph{two-hop}: $h_i=1.5f_i+2.0\\,f^{(2)}_i$, where $f^{(2)}_i$ is the treated fraction among nodes at distance exactly two (case 8).\n195:Table~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. The gated library has the lowest plane-mean error (0.140), against 0.185 for the linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction and 0.232 for a flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging. Because the evaluator's per-case and baseline values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence we use, we draw no per-case or baseline-relative conclusion from it, and in particular we do not say here whether the two alternatives beat plain linear regression. The three classes differ in several respects at once (model family, selection rule, interval construction), so this comparison supports a ranking of the three configurations on these seeds and no attribution to any single component. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping, and the final-plane error in the two-hop case remains 0.452; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. The two-hop feature in the source counts length-two walks, including walks that return to the node, and so differs from the generating distance-exactly-two fraction; whether this matters, whether the gate ever selected it, and whether the all-treated corner is poorly constrained were not tested. No ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n206: this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n213: on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, treats units as independent in the sandwich estimate even under the cluster design, and uses no resampling (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, \\texttt{sandwich\\_se}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind. Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n226:\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size.\n271:\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n279:\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n311:The constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control gives a mean absolute estimate of 0.059 times the true GATE, and the relabelled-graph error (0.627) is far above the headline error of 0.126 and similar to the difference-in-means value. We make no mechanistic claim from these values. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n314:Main-text Table~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. Only the plane-mean scores are used in this paper; per-case, bias, coverage and baseline values for this plane are not reported. The two alternatives, as implemented in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_01/program.py} and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_02/program.py}, are: (i) a flexible ensemble that fits ridge regressions on exposure features, compares candidates by cross-validation and a held-out extrapolation split, averages those within one standard error, and builds an interval from a design-resampling wild block bootstrap; and (ii) a linear-regression estimate plus a shrunk, degree-stratified HT-type residual correction with a design-based variance term. We did not inspect their per-case behaviour, so we offer no explanation for their scores.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"38f537fe-cb23-4a09-a6f2-f091f11ef09b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:51:14.376Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T08:51:03.772091+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8,\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/overview_schematic.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/overview_schematic.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        5,\n        8\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"main_accuracy\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/main_accuracy.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/main_accuracy.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_linear_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_dim_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_ht_rmse_rel\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_1\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_2\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_3\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_4\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_5\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_6\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_7\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse_rel_case_8\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        24,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"reliability\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 0.9,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 12,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/reliability.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/reliability.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.ci_coverage\",\n        \"final.metric.ci_width_rel\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n{'figure_id': 'overview_schematic', 'figure_type': 'diagram', 'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'main_accuracy', 'figure_type': 'bar_two_panel', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'reliability', 'figure_type': 'strip_plus_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n59:\\paragraph{Design-based estimation under interference.} Randomization-based frameworks define exposure conditions over a network and estimate their effects with inverse-probability weighting \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}; graph cluster randomization raises the probability that a unit's whole neighbourhood is treated together \\citep{Ugander2013GraphCluster,Ugander2020RandomizedGC}, and it has been deployed at scale \\citep{Karrer2020NetworkEA}. Subsequent work studies estimation theory under network interference \\citep{Sussman2017Elements}, the admissibility of HT estimation \\citep{Karwa2023Admissibility}, data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT \\citep{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}, interference with noisy or $k$-nearest-neighbour structure \\citep{Li2021Noise,Alzubaidi2023KNN}, and asymptotics for effect estimation under random-graph models of the network \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph}. Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\n65:\\paragraph{Regression adjustment and model-based estimation.} \\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals. Generalized propensity-score approaches model exposure probabilities \\citep{Forastiere2022EstimatingCE}, and \\citet{Shankar2024ABTU} and \\citet{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty} address estimation when the network is only partly known. Outside networks, regression adjustment is a variance-reduction tool in randomized experiments \\citep{Wager2016HighdimensionalRA,Ting2023Limits}. Our contribution relative to this literature is empirical: a controlled grid that separates cases where the fitted mapping matches the mechanism from cases where it does not, with explicit reference estimators, negative controls and an interval-calibration check. We do not derive new theory.\n83:\\item \\emph{two-hop}: $h_i=1.5f_i+2.0\\,f^{(2)}_i$, where $f^{(2)}_i$ is the treated fraction among nodes at distance exactly two (case 8).\n195:Table~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. The gated library has the lowest plane-mean error (0.140), against 0.185 for the linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction and 0.232 for a flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging. Because the evaluator's per-case and baseline values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence we use, we draw no per-case or baseline-relative conclusion from it, and in particular we do not say here whether the two alternatives beat plain linear regression. The three classes differ in several respects at once (model family, selection rule, interval construction), so this comparison supports a ranking of the three configurations on these seeds and no attribution to any single component. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping, and the final-plane error in the two-hop case remains 0.452; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. The two-hop feature in the source counts length-two walks, including walks that return to the node, and so differs from the generating distance-exactly-two fraction; whether this matters, whether the gate ever selected it, and whether the all-treated corner is poorly constrained were not tested. No ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n206: this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n213: on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, treats units as independent in the sandwich estimate even under the cluster design, and uses no resampling (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, \\texttt{sandwich\\_se}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind. Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n226:\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size.\n271:\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n279:\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n311:The constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control gives a mean absolute estimate of 0.059 times the true GATE, and the relabelled-graph error (0.627) is far above the headline error of 0.126 and similar to the difference-in-means value. We make no mechanistic claim from these values. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n314:Main-text Table~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. Only the plane-mean scores are used in this paper; per-case, bias, coverage and baseline values for this plane are not reported. The two alternatives, as implemented in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_01/program.py} and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_02/program.py}, are: (i) a flexible ensemble that fits ridge regressions on exposure features, compares candidates by cross-validation and a held-out extrapolation split, averages those within one standard error, and builds an interval from a design-resampling wild block bootstrap; and (ii) a linear-regression estimate plus a shrunk, degree-stratified HT-type residual correction with a design-based variance term. We did not inspect their per-case behaviour, so we offer no explanation for their scores.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
