{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrZWBaTfn1t9bhtTnw4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019xomeZVAZ1kRjSdiw5YkDZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\nt=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)==1,a\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\nrep(\" Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\",\" Our HT reference is of this family; it is included as an untuned design-based reference, not as a tuned competitor.\")\nrep(\"\\\\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\\\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\\\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals.\",\n\"\\\\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} study regression adjustments for estimating the global treatment effect under interference, by predicting outcomes in the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model; a related central-limit-theorem result is given by \\\\citet{Chin2018Stein}. \\\\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} propose model-based regression adjustment for network interference.\")\nrep(\"this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8.\",\"this is the mean absolute relative error over cases and is lower than the RMSE aggregate of 0.126; we did not decompose the gap by case.\")\nrep(\" Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\\\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind. Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\\\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid,\",\" Variance and interval estimation under network dependence is treated in the design-based literature \\\\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}, in work on regression adjustment \\\\citep{Han2023ModelbasedRA} and in random-graph asymptotics \\\\citep{Li2022RandomGraph}; the interval here does not follow those approaches, and we did not run any of them on this grid,\")\nrep(\"In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply\",\"In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\\\ref{tab:main}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply\")\nrep(\"a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\",\"when the outcome variance is exactly zero the estimate is 0 with a degenerate interval.\")\nrep(\"The constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control\",\"The constant-outcome control returns an estimate of magnitude about $9\\\\times10^{-15}$ (numerically zero), the permuted-outcome control\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n \"label{tab:main}\\|tab:main\" paper.tex | head -3; grep -n \"Fallbacks\\|constant\" claims_grounding.json | head; sed -i 's/dpi=300, bbox/dpi=500, bbox/' build_figures.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1648,"cache_read_input_tokens":56294,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1648},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"4f3abac6-424d-46dc-8193-761aec912124","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:51:27.068Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrZVcqqBRY4hZ4Eswqn"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019xomeZVAZ1kRjSdiw5YkDZ","type":"tool_result","content":"226:\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:main}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size.\n116:        \"final.metric.control_constant_max_abs_est\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"5503015f-7d18-4bc5-8fda-98f8619ac85b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:51:27.170Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"226:\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:main}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not va