{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012k4V1J4qpGfpuhfM93XjDd","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. A tentative practical reading is that effort spent on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family, is worth testing; this study does not isolate it. A gate of this kind also has costs we did not ablate: the choice of margin and library members is a source of researcher degrees of freedom.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:agg}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Finally, relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it summarizes small effects and heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92f14c43-e077-40f1-8a98-2a7fca78a5c1","uuid":"b6dea836-be48-4be1-8948-e7c3e3877f26","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:53:19.667Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. A tentative practical reading is that effort spent on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family, is worth testing; this study does not isolate it. A gate of this kind also has costs we did not ablate: the choice of margin and library members is a source of researcher degrees of freedom.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:agg}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Finally, relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it summarizes small effects and heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
